# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
hume-dialogues-4.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2 has "◇pleasure_suffices"; hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.2 and hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.3 have "pleasure_suffices"; they differ when only "possibly, pleasure alone, in its various degrees, is sufficient to excite all creatures to action and self-preservation" is true, which the first allows
hume-dialogues-4.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S2 is read hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.2 and hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.3 with no modality; hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means
hume-dialogues-4.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered yes, which keeps hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2, to: Suppose "possibly, pleasure alone, in its various degrees, is sufficient to excite all creatures to action and self-preservation" is true and "pleasure alone, in its various degrees, is sufficient to excite all creatures to action and self-preservation" is false. Is S2, "Now pleasure alone, in its various degrees, seems to human understanding sufficient for this purpose.", true then?
hume-dialogues-4.md:1: argue/readings: S7 is read 2 ways: hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2 has "free_hour → ◇perpetual_exemption" and "particular_contrivance"; hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.2 and hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.3 have "◇free_hour → ◇perpetual_exemption" and "particular_contrivance"; they differ when only "producing the feeling of pain required as particular a contrivance of the organs as endowing them with sight, hearing or any of the senses" and "possibly, animals are free from pain for an hour" are true, which the first allows
hume-dialogues-4.md:1: argue/answered: S7: answered no, which keeps hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.2 and hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "producing the feeling of pain required as particular a contrivance of the organs as endowing them with sight, hearing or any of the senses", "possibly, animals are free from pain for an hour" and "possibly, animals are free from pain for an hour" are true and "animals are free from pain for an hour", "possibly, animals enjoy a perpetual exemption from pain", "possibly, animals enjoy a perpetual exemption from pain" and "possibly, animals enjoy a perpetual exemption from pain" are false. Is S7, "If animals can be free from it an hour, they might enjoy a perpetual exemption from it; and it required as particular a contrivance of their organs to produce that feeling, as to endow them with sight, hearing, or any of the senses.", true then?
hume-dialogues-4.md:1: argue/readings: S8 is read 2 ways: hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2 and hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.2 has "¬reason_for_necessity"; they differ when only "there is some appearance of reason that the contrivance producing pain was necessary" is true, which the first allows
hume-dialogues-4.md:1: argue/answered: S8: answered no, which keeps hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "there is some appearance of reason that the contrivance producing pain was necessary" is true. Is S8, "Shall we conjecture, that such a contrivance was necessary, without any appearance of reason? and shall we build on that conjecture as on the most certain truth?", true then?
hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2 has "□(pleasure_suffices → life_without_pain)" and "◇life_without_pain ∧ particular_contrivance → ¬reason_for_necessity"; hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.2 has "□(diminution_prompts → life_without_pain)"; they differ when only "producing the feeling of pain required as particular a contrivance of the organs as endowing them with sight, hearing or any of the senses" and "necessarily, (if pleasure alone, in its various degrees, is sufficient to excite all creatures to action and self-preservation, then the business of life is carried on without any pain)" are true, which the first allows; hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.3 has "□(diminution_prompts → life_without_pain)" and "◇life_without_pain ∧ particular_contrivance → ¬reason_for_necessity"
hume-dialogues-4.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2 and hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.3 have "¬reason_for_necessity"; hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.2 has "◇life_without_pain"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
hume-dialogues-4.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: the conclusion is read hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2 and hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.3 with no modality; hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.2 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means
hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2 and hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.3 put it at S8, "Shall we conjecture, that such a contrivance was necessary, without any appearance of reason? and shall we build on that conjecture as on the most certain truth?"; hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.2 at S5, "It seems, therefore, plainly possible to carry on the business of life without any pain."
hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2: argue/answered: M: answered yes, which keeps hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2 and hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.3, to: Is the text's main conclusion S8, "Shall we conjecture, that such a contrivance was necessary, without any appearance of reason? and shall we build on that conjecture as on the most certain truth?", rather than S5, "It seems, therefore, plainly possible to carry on the business of life without any pain."?
hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2 takes for granted "necessarily, (if pleasure alone, in its various degrees, is sufficient to excite all creatures to action and self-preservation, then the business of life is carried on without any pain)"; hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.2 and hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.3 do not
hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered no, which keeps hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.2 and hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "necessarily, (if pleasure alone, in its various degrees, is sufficient to excite all creatures to action and self-preservation, then the business of life is carried on without any pain)"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: U2: hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.2 and hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.3 take for granted "necessarily, (if when urged by the necessities of nature, animals feel a diminution of pleasure instead of pain, which prompts them to seek what their subsistence needs, then the business of life is carried on without any pain)"; hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2 does not
hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.2 and hume-dialogues-4.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "necessarily, (if when urged by the necessities of nature, animals feel a diminution of pleasure instead of pain, which prompts them to seek what their subsistence needs, then the business of life is carried on without any pain)"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
